Historian Elena Marchetti argues that the collapse of the Western Roman Empire was fundamentally an economic failure. She contends that the empire''s reliance on slave labor suppressed technological innovation, leading to long-term stagnation. As the supply of slaves dwindled, the economy could not adapt, and the resulting fiscal weakness made the empire vulnerable to external pressures. Political and military crises, in her view, were symptoms of this underlying economic decay.

