Historian David Okonkwo maintains that the empire''s downfall was primarily a military catastrophe. He points to the progressive reliance on non-citizen mercenaries who lacked loyalty to Rome, combined with the unsustainable cost of defending overextended frontiers. While acknowledging that economic troubles existed, he argues that they were consequences of repeated military failures, not their cause.', 'Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1 that economic decay was the "root cause" of the Western Roman Empire''s collapse?', '["By arguing that economic decay was a result of military failures rather than the fundamental cause.","By agreeing that economic factors were paramount but emphasizing the role of slave labor.","By dismissing economic explanations as entirely irrelevant to the empire''s fall.","By asserting that the empire''s size, not its economy or military, was the sole factor in its collapse."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Text 2 explicitly states that economic troubles were consequences of military failures, not their cause. Therefore, the author of Text 2 would respond to the claim that economic decay was the root cause by arguing that it was instead a result of military failures. The other options misrepresent Text 2: it does not agree that economic factors were paramount, it does not dismiss economic explanations entirely, and it does not claim that size alone was responsible.', 'ai', true, 'hard', 'model', 'mcq', null, null, null, 'active', '29dc1a2f61c1ded4c010c5a60d712cb4c33a03f32f0aa09397d70659e0c1c6e4') on conflict (id) do nothing;
The decline of the Classic Maya civilization in the ninth century CE has often been attributed to a prolonged drought that caused agricultural collapse. Paleoclimatic data from lake sediments indicate a severe dry period coinciding with the abandonment of major urban centers. This evidence suggests that environmental factors were the decisive cause of the civilization''s downfall.

