While drought undoubtedly stressed Maya society, focusing solely on climate ignores the role of endemic warfare and elite competition. Archaeological findings reveal that many cities were already experiencing political fragmentation and overpopulation before the drought intensified. Thus, the collapse was a complex process in which social instability was the primary driver, with drought acting as a contributing but not sufficient factor.', 'Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1 that drought was the decisive cause of the Maya collapse?', '["By conceding that drought played a role but emphasizing that it was not the sole or primary cause.","By rejecting the idea that any drought occurred during the period of decline.","By arguing that political strife was a direct consequence of the drought.","By claiming that overpopulation was the only factor responsible for the collapse."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Text 2 acknowledges that drought ''undoubtedly stressed Maya society'' but argues that social instability was the primary driver and that drought was merely a contributing factor. Thus, the author would concede drought''s role while emphasizing it was not the decisive cause, making the correct choice the one that states this nuanced position.', 'ai', true, 'hard', 'model', 'mcq', null, null, null, 'active', '2c4c0a2fc244c872f6e6902ab5a895a79269299f64c76d6931655443d14f2ba5') on conflict (id) do nothing;
Social media platforms employ algorithms that personalize content based on user behavior, creating ''filter bubbles'' that reinforce existing beliefs. Research indicates that these algorithms limit exposure to opposing viewpoints, thereby intensifying political polarization. As users encounter only information that aligns with their preferences, they become more entrenched in their ideological positions.

